#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106903} :activity/id #uuid "70093123-9441-504b-9d19-1ff886bae1a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485 :topic/id #uuid "f8b72b93-d1e8-5602-88e3-d4c903a3465a" :topic/math-academy-id 1376} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107157 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a point $P$ with position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ and another vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ \n\n![](images/b1/b1bb8d0cbe5dcbb24e224e8efc77e32db7a1657b245666aebd7b18107ee41149.png)\n\nThe **vector equation** of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=p+tv,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is often called the **direction vector** of the line, while the vector $\\mathbf{r}$ denotes the position vector of an arbitrary point on the line.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe vector equation of the line  that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5bf1db5d-9d49-578a-b4a3-de51006d5ffd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7711 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "da72d9c9-d225-5f92-acf1-85ac68f4a4ea" :step/math-academy-id 44724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105463 :question/id #uuid "9fa21896-8127-5ec5-aff0-7f811403db8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12442"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390434}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"} :step/id #uuid "cec448b9-8490-5eef-b44a-d69c60421dab" :step/math-academy-id 44725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105464 :question/id #uuid "d1adb6fb-cd44-5a12-b892-6a6d2c99929d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12443"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393702}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points"} :step/id #uuid "a9e901bc-5996-547e-9050-8a27e0a73211" :step/math-academy-id 44728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107158 :tutorial/content "There is a geometric intuition behind the formula $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/93/93b062a23c613184e7cdf725852401168b609a32056a5efcd24ceca2703d81d2.png)\n\nTo get to any point $A(x,y,z)$ on the desired line, starting from the origin $O,$ we can\n\n- first travel along the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ to get to the point $P$ on the line, and\n\n- then travel along the vector $t \\mathbf{v}$ to get to $A.$ (The scalar $t$ represents how far we travel forward or backward along the line in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$)" :tutorial/id #uuid "24f7081a-5ffa-545e-9268-8abff5267044" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7712 :tutorial/title "Geometric Intuition Behind the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "1882d34e-68eb-5861-b83e-9a6271a116a2" :step/math-academy-id 44731 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105465 :question/id #uuid "55ee3b10-087f-521e-ba37-12541e199408" :question/math-academy-id "e-12444"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403871}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"} :step/id #uuid "7ec783db-e49b-5e4a-a359-af68f2d97438" :step/math-academy-id 44732 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105466 :question/id #uuid "69c3fc44-8b4d-521e-b207-0ea01d5921e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12445"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281402}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"} :step/id #uuid "32a3a6c0-a3b3-531d-881f-278b4030a495" :step/math-academy-id 44735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107159 :tutorial/content "![](images/44/443bbe1d201f1919fa2fb7ce3b632715cdc0e8dc77ff94bdc89f2cff7402b772.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find a vector equation of the line *segment* whose endpoints are $P(3,-1,7)$ and $Q(3,4,4).$ \n\nTo do this, we need to find the vector equation of the corresponding line and then restrict the domain of the parameter $t.$\n\nFirst, we need to find a direction vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis equation describes every point on the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}.$ \n\nTo restrict this to points on the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ only, we set $t\\in [0,1].$ Thus, the vector equation of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWriting the equation of a line segment in this way is sometimes called **parametrizing** the line segment since each point on the segment corresponds to a single parameter $t.$\n\n- Notice that $t=0$ gives us the position vector of $P{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5(0)-1 \\\\ 7-3(0)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Similarly, $t=1$ gives us the position vector of $Q{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5(1)-1 \\\\ 7-3(1)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince increasing $t$ moves our vector $\\mathbf r (t)$ from $P$ to $Q,$ we say that the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is **traversed** *from* the point $P$ *to* the point $Q$ under this parametrization." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ec7269d-cea3-5a82-818b-cb048217f704" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7713 :tutorial/title "The Vector Equation of a Segment"} :step/id #uuid "50416523-59b6-572d-9328-3ca6a25a4c94" :step/math-academy-id 44736 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106908}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106003}]}
